bridge	P5	live well	performing the soul's function (living) excellently	faring well, being blessed and happy
# In A1, A2 and C1, "live well/ill" is drawn from the soul doing its function (life) well or badly through its excellence. P5 takes the same phrase in the everyday sense of faring well or being happy. That carries the conclusion from functioning to happiness.
# I was less sure about "excellence" (functional excellence vs. moral justice, P3/P4) and "profitable" (advantage in Thrasymachus's sense vs. what is good for one). In both cases each claim keeps one sense across the statements that use it.
